export default {"id":"bassstation-level-9","number":9,"title":"Analogue Filters (Classic LP/BP/HP, Acid & Overdrive)","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Contrast the physical circuit topologies of the Classic state-variable filter and the Acid diode-ladder filter. - Master all multi-mode configurations of the Classic filter: Low-Pass (LP), Band-Pass (BP), and High-Pass (HP) at both 12 dB ","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-9-1","code":"9","title":"Analogue Filters (Classic LP/BP/HP, Acid & Overdrive)","blurb":"- Contrast the physical circuit topologies of the Classic state-variable filter and the Acid diode-ladder filter. - Master all multi-mode configurations of the Classic filter: Low-Pass (LP), Band-Pass (BP), and High-Pass (HP) at both 12 dB ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-9","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 9.1 — Dual-Topology Analogue Filter Section","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Contrast the physical circuit topologies of the <strong>Classic</strong> state-variable filter and the <strong>Acid</strong> diode-ladder filter.","Master all multi-mode configurations of the Classic filter: Low-Pass (<strong>LP</strong>), Band-Pass (<strong>BP</strong>), and High-Pass (<strong>HP</strong>) at both <strong>12 dB</strong> and <strong>24 dB/octave</strong> slopes.","Control filter cutoff frequency, resonance feedback, and evaluate self-oscillation boundaries safely.","Apply pre-filter analogue <strong>Overdrive</strong> to introduce harmonic density and saturation before filter resonance peaks.","Calibrate dynamic filter cutoff modulation using <strong>Mod Env</strong> and <strong>LFO 2</strong>, applying exact semitone/octave landmark depth values.","Configure and evaluate 8-stage <strong>Filter Keyboard Tracking</strong> (<code>F-0</code> to <code>F-7</code>)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-1","title":"9.1.1: The Acid Diode-Ladder Filter Topology","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II incorporates two completely distinct physical analog filter circuits. The <strong>Acid</strong> filter is a discrete 4-pole diode-ladder low-pass design inspired by the iconic 1980s Roland TB-303 baseline synthesizer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      ACID DIODE-LADDER TOPOLOGY\n   [Mixer Sum] ──► [Overdrive] ──► [4-Pole Diode Ladder] ──► [Distortion / VCA]\n                                           ▲\n                                           │ (Non-linear Resonant Feedback)\n                                   [Resonance Pot]\n   *Operational Constraints*:\n   • Permanent 24 dB/octave (4-pole) Low-Pass response.\n   • [Shape] Switch (LP/BP/HP) is COMPLETELY BYPASSED &amp; INOPERATIVE.\n   • [Slope] Switch (12dB/24dB) is COMPLETELY BYPASSED &amp; INOPERATIVE.</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Non-Linear Resonance Behavior</strong>:","In standard linear state-variable filters, turning up resonance causes a noticeable reduction in low-frequency bass energy (passband drop).","In the Acid diode-ladder circuit, increasing resonance introduces non-linear saturation that warms and squelches the signal, producing the characteristic aggressive, biting, \"liquid\" resonance squelch defining acid house, techno, and electro basslines.","<strong>Control Lockout Architecture</strong>:","When <strong>Type</strong> is set to <strong>Acid</strong>, the <strong>Shape</strong> switch (LP/BP/HP) and <strong>Slope</strong> switch (12 dB/24 dB) are physically disconnected from the circuit path.","The Acid filter is <strong>permanently a 24 dB/octave Low-Pass filter</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. Set <strong>Type</strong> to <strong>Acid</strong>.","Rotate <strong>Resonance</strong> to approximately 80% (value ~100).","Sweep the <strong>Frequency</strong> dial while playing sixteenth notes. Listen to the distinct non-linear resonance squelch.","Flip the <strong>Shape</strong> switch between LP, BP, and HP, and toggle <strong>Slope</strong> between 12 dB and 24 dB.","Confirm that the sound remains strictly 24 dB Low-Pass regardless of Shape and Slope switch positions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the slope and filter shape of the Acid filter circuit?","[ ] Why do the Shape and Slope toggle switches have no effect when the Filter Type is set to Acid?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-2","title":"9.1.2: The Classic State-Variable Multi-Mode Filter","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Classic</strong> filter is a highly flexible, multi-mode state-variable analog filter topology offering six distinct filtering combinations:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                  CLASSIC STATE-VARIABLE MULTI-MODE MATRIX                  │\n├───────────────┬───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Configuration │ Slope / Attenuation       │ Acoustic Character &amp; Use Case  │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic LP 12 │ 2-Pole (12 dB / Octave)   │ Warm, open, bright rolloff;    │\n│               │                           │ preserves top-end harmonics.   │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic LP 24 │ 4-Pole (24 dB / Octave)   │ Steep, punchy, aggressive      │\n│               │                           │ rolloff; thick modern bass.    │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic BP 12 │ 2-Pole (6 dB/oct per side)│ Broad vocal/formant bandpass;  │\n│               │                           │ wide mid-frequency focus.      │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic BP 24 │ 4-Pole (12dB/oct per side)│ Narrow, resonant, nasal formant│\n│               │                           │ tracking; radio/telephone FX.  │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic HP 12 │ 2-Pole (12 dB / Octave)   │ Gentle sub-bass cut; brightens │\n│               │                           │ leads, percussive hats/shakers.│\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Classic HP 24 │ 4-Pole (24 dB / Octave)   │ Surgical high-pass; aggressive │\n│               │                           │ low-end elimination.           │\n└───────────────┴───────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Shape Modes</strong>:","<strong>LP (Low Pass)</strong>: Attenuates high frequencies above cutoff; passes fundamental and low harmonics.","<strong>BP (Band Pass)</strong>: Rejects both high and low frequencies; passes a centered band. The Frequency dial sets center frequency.","<strong>HP (High Pass)</strong>: Attenuates low frequencies below cutoff; passes upper harmonics.","<strong>Slope Modes</strong>:","<strong>12 dB (2-Pole)</strong>: Gentle attenuation slope. Perfect for vintage 1970s funk leads, brass swells, and organic pads.","<strong>24 dB (4-Pole)</strong>: Sharp, steep attenuation slope. Perfect for modern electronic sub-bass, hard electro plucks, and razor-sharp filter sweeps."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Type to <strong>Classic</strong>, Shape to <strong>LP</strong>, and Slope to <strong>12 dB</strong>. Play a saw bass; note the bright harmonic sheen.","Toggle Slope to <strong>24 dB</strong>; note the immediate dark, punchy tightening of the low end.","Switch Shape to <strong>BP</strong> (24 dB); sweep the Frequency dial to hear telephone/vocal formant resonances.","Switch Shape to <strong>HP</strong>; sweep Frequency clockwise; observe the complete removal of low-frequency bass energy."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-3","title":"9.1.3: Filter Frequency and Resonance Self-Oscillation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Filter Frequency Potentiometer</strong>:","High-resolution oversized analog dial governing the cutoff frequency across the full audible spectrum (from sub-audio &lt; 20 Hz up to &gt; 20 kHz).","<strong>Resonance Feedback &amp; Self-Oscillation</strong>:","Potentiometer scales the positive feedback gain around the filter core.","As Resonance approaches values of <strong>95–127</strong>, the feedback loop energy exceeds internal damping, and the filter enters <strong>pure sine-wave self-oscillation</strong>.","When self-oscillating, the filter becomes a pitch-accurate acoustic oscillator capable of being played via keyboard tracking, even with all mixer sources muted."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In the mixer, turn <strong>all six sources to zero</strong> (silence).","Set Filter to <strong>Classic</strong>, <strong>LP</strong>, <strong>24 dB</strong>.","Turn <strong>Resonance</strong> fully clockwise (127).","Sweep the <strong>Frequency</strong> knob; hear a pure, high-amplitude sine wave generated entirely by the self-oscillating filter."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-4","title":"9.1.4: Pre-Filter Overdrive Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Overdrive</strong> control is a dedicated discrete analog saturation circuit placed <strong>directly before the filter input</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[Mixer Summing Bus] ──► [PRE-FILTER OVERDRIVE] ──► [Analogue Filter Core] ──► [Post Distortion]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pre-Filter Dynamics</strong>:","Overdrive amplifies the mixed audio signal into soft analog clipping before it reaches the filter resonance stage.","Because it saturates the signal <em>before</em> the filter, it generates rich upper harmonics that give the filter cutoff and resonance significantly more spectral material to bite into and sweep through.","<strong>Distinction from Post-Distortion</strong>:","Pre-filter <strong>Overdrive</strong> creates smooth, warm, creamy saturation that changes dynamically as the filter opens and closes.","Post-filter <strong>Distortion</strong> (Module 11) adds hard, gritty fuzz and clipping after the filter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load an Init patch. Set Filter Frequency to 50% with Resonance at 70%.","Slowly rotate <strong>Overdrive</strong> from 0 to 127.","Listen to the subtle increase in harmonic warmth, aggressive mid-range growl, and dense saturation."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-5","title":"9.1.5: Dynamic Filter Cutoff Modulation (Mod Env & LFO 2)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Filter section features two dedicated bipolar rotary potentiometers for real-time cutoff modulation:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  FILTER CUTOFF MODULATION DEPTHS\n ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │ [Mod Env Depth Pot] ──► Cutoff Frequency Envelope Modulation            │\n │   • Center (0) = Neutral / Inactive                                      │\n │   • Clockwise (+1 to +127) = Positive Envelope (Filter opens on attack)  │\n │   • Counter-Clockwise (-1 to -127) = Inverted Envelope (Filter closes)   │\n ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n │ [LFO 2 Depth Pot] ──► Cyclic Cutoff Modulation via LFO 2 (Wobble/Auto-Wah)│\n │   • Center (0) = Inactive                                                │\n │   • Value (0 to 127) = Depth of cyclic filter cutoff oscillation         │\n └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mod Env Cutoff Sweep</strong>:","Routes Envelope 2 (Mod Env) to dynamically open or close the filter over time.","Positive depths create classic synth brass swells, plucks, and acid sweeps.","Inverted (negative) depths pull the cutoff frequency downwards on note strike.","<strong>Authoritative Landmark Depth Values (User Guide Calibration)</strong>:","When modulating Filter Frequency via Mod Env, use the following calibrated display values:","Value <strong><code>1</code></strong> = <strong>76 Cents</strong> (Micro-tonal timbral shift)","Value <strong><code>16</code></strong> = <strong>1 Full Octave (12 Semitones) cutoff sweep</strong>","Value <strong><code>32</code></strong> = <strong>2 Full Octaves (24 Semitones) cutoff sweep</strong>","Value <strong><code>64</code></strong> = <strong>4 Full Octaves cutoff sweep</strong>","Value <strong><code>127</code></strong> = <strong>Full-Band Cutoff Modulation (Maximum dynamic sweep)</strong>","<strong>LFO 2 to Filter</strong>:","Dedicated routing from LFO 2 to modulate filter cutoff.","Produces classic cyclic auto-wah, subtle acoustic filter breathing, or tempo-synced dubstep/drum-and-bass \"wobble\" basses."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Close <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> to 0 (fully counter-clockwise).","Set Mod Env Attack to 0, Decay to 50%, Sustain to 0, Release to 0.","Set <strong>Mod Env</strong> filter depth to exactly <strong><code>32</code></strong>.","Play a note; observe the filter snap open by exactly two octaves on note strike and decay smoothly back to silence.","Set Mod Env depth to 0, open Frequency to 50%, and turn <strong>LFO 2</strong> depth to 75% with LFO 2 set to Triangle; hear cyclic auto-wah modulation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What exact Mod Env depth value produces an exact 1-octave cutoff frequency sweep?","[ ] What is the display value for an exact 2-octave cutoff frequency sweep?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-9-1-6","title":"9.1.6: Filter Keyboard Tracking","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.6: Filter Keyboard Tracking","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"prose","html":"Filter Keyboard Tracking scales the filter cutoff frequency in direct proportion to the pitch of the note played on the keybed."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    FILTER KEYBOARD TRACKING PARAMETERS                     │\n├───────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Setting       │ Tracking Ratio &amp; Acoustic Behavior                         │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `F-0`         │ 100% Tracking (Full 1:1 pitch-to-cutoff tracking). Default │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `F-1` to `F-6`│ Fractional Tracking (Cutoff opens at reduced ratios).      │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `F-7`         │ 0% Tracking (Filter cutoff completely static across keys). │\n└───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Acoustic Rationale</strong>:","At <strong>100% Tracking (<code>F-0</code>)</strong>, the filter cutoff frequency moves up and down in exact lockstep with keyboard pitch, ensuring consistent brightness and harmonic balance across all registers.","At <strong>0% Tracking (<code>F-7</code>)</strong>, the filter cutoff remains fixed at a single frequency regardless of which key is struck. Lower notes sound bright and open, while higher notes are increasingly filtered and muted.","<strong>Self-Oscillation Keyboard Playing</strong>:","To play the self-oscillating filter as a pitch-accurate musical instrument, Filter Tracking <strong>must be set to <code>F-0</code> (100% tracking)</strong>.","<strong>Configuration Gesture</strong>:","Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong>, then <strong>press the Filter Freq key twice</strong> (the upper <code>F</code> key).","Display indicates <code>F-0</code> through <code>F-7</code>. Adjust using <strong>Patch/Value ▲ and ▼</strong>.","Setting is saved per-patch into memory."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["Set Filter Tracking to <code>F-0</code>. Turn Resonance to 127 with all mixer sources muted.","Play musical scales on the keyboard; hear the self-oscillating filter play accurate musical pitches.","Access the menu and change Filter Tracking to <code>F-7</code>.","Play the keyboard; observe that striking different keys produces the exact same single static pitch. Reset back to <code>F-0</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate E — Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to Module 10, verify complete mastery of Gate E proficiencies:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] <strong>Dual Topology Comparison</strong>: Demonstrate Classic LP 12, Classic LP 24, and Acid filter modes on the same sawtooth bassline, explaining the acoustic and circuit differences.","[ ] <strong>Multi-Mode Operation</strong>: Demonstrate Classic Band-Pass (BP) and High-Pass (HP) modes with appropriate frequency sweeps.","[ ] <strong>Pre-Filter Overdrive</strong>: Demonstrate the acoustic impact of Overdrive on harmonic density before the filter cutoff.","[ ] <strong>Modulation Calibration</strong>: Demonstrate setting Mod Env depth to landmark values <code>16</code> (1 octave) and <code>32</code> (2 octaves).","[ ] <strong>Filter Tracking Verification</strong>: Access the <code>F-0…F-7</code> menu using the double-tap gesture, explain the difference between full tracking (<code>F-0</code>) and zero tracking (<code>F-7</code>), and restore it to <code>F-0</code>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
